 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method avoids nested iterations over all pixels and all Gaussians by using bounding box rejection and tile-based distribution [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Supports up to 2 million Gaussians with respectable reconstruction times on a single NVIDIA RTX A4000 GPU [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Measured on NVIDIA RTX A4000 GPU [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient handling of 3D ultrasound volumes; avoids occlusion modeling; stable optimization via triangular covariance parameterization [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires custom CUDA kernels for rasterization and gradients; initialization is random rather than point-cloud derived [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $N$ and the number of pixels $P$ covered by the bounding boxes. The rejection phase is $O(N)$. The rasterization phase is $O(\sum_{i \in \text{accepted}} \text{pixels}_i)$. With efficient bounding box culling, this is significantly faster than naive $O(N \cdot P)$.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store Gaussian parameters. $O(P)$ for the output buffers.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Tested on NVIDIA RTX A4000 GPU. Supports up to 2 million Gaussians with respectable reconstruction times. 100k, 200k, and 2M Gaussians variants were tested [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Avoids occlusion modeling errors inherent in projecting 3D Gaussians to 2D for ultrasound. Efficient CUDA implementation.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires 3D Mahalanobis distance calculation per pixel, which is more expensive than 2D projection. Numerical stability requires careful parameterization of covariance.
+*   **When to use:** 3D ultrasound volume reconstruction.
+*   **When not to use:** Standard RGB image rendering where occlusion is important.
 
 ## Background
-UltraGauss was introduced in the paper "UltraGauss: Ultrafast Gaussian Reconstruction of 3D Ultrasound Volumes" [S1, unofficial]. It adapts 3D Gaussian Splatting for the specific domain of ultrasound imaging, where the probe plane intersects the volume rather than projecting it [S1, unofficial]. The method was implemented in PyTorch with custom CUDA kernels [S1, unofficial].
+UltraGauss was introduced in the paper "UltraGauss: Ultrafast Gaussian Reconstruction of 3D Ultrasound Volumes" [S1, unofficial]. It adapts 3D Gaussian Splatting for the specific domain of ultrasound, where the probe plane intersects the volume rather than projecting it. The method was developed to address the limitations of standard Gaussian Splatting in ultrasound, specifically the incorrect modeling of occlusions and the lack of interior volume representation from surface-only point clouds.
 
